        "original_id": "9f659b4a-744e-56a3-8d00-16468c16b41e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Johnny Majors, Paul Hornung, Antone Davis\n- Organizations: Tennessee Volunteers, Green Bay Packers, Southeastern Conference, National Football League\n- Places: Neyland Stadium, Knoxville, Tennessee\n- Events: Heisman Trophy voting, 1990 Tennessee Volunteers football season, Super Bowl\n- Roles: Head coach, running back, offensive tackle\n\nFacts:\n- In 1956, Johnny Majors finished second to Paul Hornung in voting for the Heisman Trophy.\n- Paul Hornung was born on December 23, 1935.\n- From 1957 to 1966, Paul Hornung played professional American football as a Hall of Fame running back for the Green Bay Packers of the National Football League.\n- During his Green Bay Packers career, Paul Hornung won four NFL titles and the first Super Bowl.\n- Before his junior year as a Tennessee Volunteer, Antone Davis was moved to right offensive tackle.\n- In 1990, Antone Davis earned unanimous All-American honors as a senior for Tennessee.\n- In 1990, the Tennessee Volunteers football team played as a member of the Southeastern Conference.\n- In 1990, Johnny Majors served as the Tennessee Volunteers football team head coach in his 14th year.\n- In 1990, the Tennessee Volunteers football team played its home games at Neyland Stadium in Knoxville, Tennessee.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
